# Signal synth project conventions

This codebase is a Web Audio synthesizer + drum machine built as standalone
React/TypeScript components (no audio libraries — raw Web Audio API only).
Follow these established patterns when extending it.

## Ref-mirroring for audio callbacks
State used inside `useCallback`s that must stay stable (e.g. `triggerVoice`,
`polyNoteOn`) is NOT read directly from React state — it's mirrored into a
`useRef` on every render (e.g. `attackRef.current = attack`), and the
callback reads `.current`. This avoids stale closures without forcing the
callback to be recreated on every param change (which would restart
scheduler `useEffect`s that depend on it and glitch playback). When adding
a new synth parameter used inside a callback, add both the state AND a
mirrored ref, following the existing examples.

## Permanent audio graph, gain-based bypass
Effects (Delay, Reverb, Chorus, filter blend) are wired ONCE and left
connected permanently. Toggling something "off" ramps its wet gain to 0
and dry gain to 1 — nothing is ever disconnected/reconnected at runtime.
This avoids audio clicks and graph-rebuild complexity. Follow this pattern
for any new effect rather than dynamically connecting/disconnecting nodes.

## Mono vs poly voice architecture
- Mono: a persistent, never-recreated oscillator pool (fixed pool size
  `MAX_UNISON`), reused across every note. Live-updatable mid-note.
- Poly: oscillators/filters are created fresh per note-on and torn down
  (with `onended` cleanup + explicit `AudioParam` disconnects) per note-off.
  NOT live-updatable mid-hold — new settings only apply to the next note.
This asymmetry is intentional, not a bug — keep it consistent when adding
new per-voice features (sub-osc, noise layer, filter blend all follow it).

## The "patch" system
`SynthPatch` (in Synth.tsx) is the saveable/randomizable unit. It covers
sound-shaping parameters ONLY — waveform, envelopes, filter, effects. It
deliberately excludes: play mode (Keys/Arp/Seq), arp settings, sequencer
pattern, and skin. Loading or randomizing a patch should never change
those. When adding a new patch field:
1. Add it to the `SynthPatch` interface.
2. Add it to `INIT_PATCH` and every factory preset in `FACTORY_PRESETS`.
3. Wire it in both `applyPatch` and `capturePatch`.
4. If you're only adding a field (new default applies fine to old saved
   data), no storage version bump needed — `sanitizePatch` backfills
   missing fields from `INIT_PATCH` automatically.
5. If you're changing the *content* of existing factory presets (not just
   adding a field), bump `PRESET_STORAGE_KEY` (e.g. v5 -> v6) — otherwise
   users' already-cached localStorage data will shadow your new preset
   values silently. See the version history in that constant for examples
   of when this was and wasn't necessary.

## Known follow-ups
See the "Worthwhile follow-ups" block in Synth.tsx's top comment for the
running list of intentionally-deferred features (stereo width/panning,
MIDI input, factory/user preset separation, etc.) before assuming
something is missing by accident.

## Multi-instrument sync
Synth and DrumMachine each run independent `setInterval`-based schedulers
and independent `AudioContext`s. They support an optional controlled `bpm`
prop + `bpmLocked` (see StudioExample.tsx) for tempo sync, but this is
NOT sample-accurate lockstep — just periodic realignment on BPM change.
Don't assume tighter sync than that without deliberately building a shared
audio clock.
